Title :
ecoLogic - Turning Agricultural Waste into Smart Biomaterials

Methodology :
Agricultural waste such as rice and wheat straw is processed and inoculated with mycelium, then molded into eco-bricks and biodegradable packaging. Arduino-based sensors continuously monitor temperature, humidity, and moisture, generating cultivation data for machine-learning analysis. The ML system can identify growth patterns, predict optimal conditions, and support material-quality optimization. After colonization, the products are dried to create lightweight, durable biomaterials.

Problem :
India generates nearly 500 million tonnes of crop residue annually, with about 92 million tonnes burned in open fields. This practice releases harmful pollutants, degrades air quality, and contributes to climate change. Meanwhile, construction and packaging industries continue to rely on resource-intensive, non-biodegradable materials. A sustainable solution is needed to convert agricultural waste into valuable eco-friendly products.

Scientific Approach/Concept :
ecoLogic uses mycelium biofabrication, where fungal mycelium binds agricultural waste into lightweight, biodegradable biomaterials. IoT sensors monitor temperature, humidity, and moisture, generating data for machine-learning analysis to identify growth patterns, predict optimal conditions, and support improved material quality.

Objective :
The objective of ecoLogic is to transform agricultural waste into sustainable mycelium-based eco-bricks and biodegradable packaging materials. In real-world applications, these products can be used for eco-friendly construction, thermal insulation, protective packaging, and waste reduction initiatives. The project aims to reduce crop residue burning, lower environmental pollution, promote a circular economy, and provide affordable green alternatives to conventional materials.

Results :
Positive Impacts: Reduces agricultural waste burning, lowers air pollution and carbon emissions, promotes sustainable construction and packaging, and supports a circular economy through biodegradable materials.
Disadvantages: Mycelium products require controlled growth conditions and have longer production times than conventional materials, which may affect large-scale manufacturing and adoption.

Target population :
Farmers, construction and packaging industries, eco-conscious consumers, and communities worldwide.

Conclusion :
ecoLogic transforms low-cost agricultural waste into high-value biodegradable biomaterials. IoT-based monitoring and machine-learning-driven growth optimization can enhance production consistency, quality, and scalability. With abundant raw materials and rising demand for sustainable packaging, construction materials, ecoLogic has strong potential for large-scale adoption and commercial growth.

Social impacts :
ecoLogic reduces pollution from crop burning, creates sustainable products, supports environmental conservation, and promotes healthier, cleaner communities through responsible waste utilization.

